- ONCHIGH (the ONC high-priority drug-drug interaction list) — Phansalkar et al., 2012,
  and the Ayvaz/Boyce 2015 update, identified under a U.S. Office of the National
  Coordinator for Health IT (ONC) contract performed by RAND Corporation, whose
  government-contract terms grant the U.S. government an irrevocable licence to reuse
  the work. drugref's own determination does not rest on that licence alone: the drug
  pairs the papers identify are facts, and facts are not copyrightable (Feist
  Publications, Inc. v. Rural Telephone Service Co., 499 U.S. 340 (1991)). drugref
  re-encodes the interactions, never the papers' text — no verbatim text from either
  paper enters any field, every `mechanism` and `management` string is authored by
  drugref itself, and each entry cites its source paper so provenance is inspectable.
  See "The ONC high-priority floor is facts, not text"
  (https://docs.drugref.org/decisions/the-onc-high-priority-floor/) for the full
  argument.
- DrugCentral `ddi` (drug–drug interactions) — DrugCentral is published by the Division
  of Translational Informatics, University of New Mexico, under CC BY-SA 4.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/). That licence covers DrugCentral's
  compilation and is not evidence of a right to relicense a third-party compendium
  inside it, so the determination was made per reference, read out of the dump's own
  `reference` table rather than inferred. drugref ingests ONE of the three references
  the `ddi` table cites: `ddi_ref_id = 2`, the U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs /
  Veterans Health Administration National Drug File – Reference Terminology (NDF-RT),
  a work of the U.S. federal government. The other two — Stockley's Drug Interactions
  (Karen Baxter, 2010, ISBN 0853699143, a copyrighted book) and Lexicomp Online
  (Wolters Kluwer Health, a commercial compendium) — are NOT ingested. Because `2` is
  a surrogate key in a dump that has been published once, the constant is not trusted
  alone: the orchestrator reads the `reference` row for every admitted id and ABORTS
  the ingest unless its recorded authors and title still match, so a re-published dump
  that renumbered its references stops rather than bundling an excluded one. Source
  release: drugcentral.dump.11012023.sql.gz, SHA-256
  055904d152d6c8eef4ee872b25f6476019682df8b5f49bcdf7cc018204f3e04f, recorded on
  ingest_run.source_checksum for every load. The committed fixture
  (tests/fixtures/drugcentral_ddi_subset.sql.gz, built by
  tests/fixtures/make_drugcentral_subset.py) carries rows from ALL THREE references so
  the exclusion is exercised against a dump that really contains what it excludes, and
  the excluded references' `description` text is redacted, following the MED-RT
  fixture's endpoint redaction. `description` is the ONLY column redacted, so the rest
  of each excluded row is committed as it stands; what that amounts to is set out here
  field by field, because a licence notice that miscounts the artefact it accounts for
  is worth nothing. Two of the seven columns are numbers with no expressive content at
  all (`id`, the dump's surrogate row key, and `ddi_ref_id`, the reference number rule 6
  turns on). The other four carry text — `drug_class1`, `drug_class2`, `ddi_risk` and
  `source_id` — and each was cleared BEFORE the fixture was committed rather than
  justified afterwards, on two grounds that run through all four: short words and
  phrases are categorically outside copyright protection whatever went into them
  (37 C.F.R. §202.1(a), "words and short phrases such as names, titles, and slogans"),
  and what these fields express is fact rather than expression, unprotectable under
  Feist Publications, Inc. v. Rural Telephone Service Co., 499 U.S. 340 (1991) — the
  same argument this file already makes for ONCHIGH. Applied field by field:
  `drug_class1`/`drug_class2` are the two endpoint names ("Monoamine Oxidase
  Inhibitors", "buspirone"), and together they state the fact that a compendium pairs
  those two agents. `source_id` carries that compendium's own monograph heading in
  readable form ("MAOIs or RIMAs + Buspirone" from Stockley's, "Conivaptan: CYP3A4
  Substrates" from Lexicomp) — three-to-five-word noun phrases naming a drug/class
  pairing, and restating the same pairing fact the two endpoint columns already carry.
  `ddi_risk` is a one- or two-word severity band drawn from the dump's own closed
  five-value vocabulary ("Critical", "Significant", "Potentially significant",
  "Contraindicated", "Avoid combination"); on the excluded rows it is the excluded
  compendium's label rather than the VHA's, which is why it is assessed here and not
  waved through with the ref-2 content. It is cleared on both grounds together: a
  one-word band leaves no room for authorship and is a short phrase under §202.1(a),
  and what it conveys is the fact of how that compendium graded the pair, which Feist
  puts outside protection. The four excluded rows carry three distinct bands between
  them, so no part of the vocabulary's own selection or arrangement is reproduced
  either. The full reasoning is written out in
  tests/fixtures/make_drugcentral_subset.py's docstring; a future regeneration that
  selects an excluded row whose `source_id` is free-form prose rather than a heading,
  or whose `ddi_risk` is anything other than a band from that closed vocabulary, is NOT
  covered by it and needs the same redaction `description` gets.

- SPL section 34073-7 (DRUG INTERACTIONS) — the structured product labeling every US
  prescription drug carries, read from two publishers of the same documents. The bulk
  `drug/label` export from the U.S. Food and Drug Administration's openFDA
  (https://open.fda.gov/) is dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/); the same labels are published by
  the U.S. National Library of Medicine as DailyMed (https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/),
  which disclaims — "cannot guarantee the copyright status for any item" — over labeling
  "submitted to the FDA by companies". Because the two publishers take opposite positions
  on the same bytes, the determination was made PER COLUMN rather than per source:

    * entity occurrences and their character offsets are FACTS and are not copyrightable
      (Feist), and are stored for every recognised drug name;
    * a `set_id` / `version` / `effective_time` citation is a citation and not a copy,
      and is stored for every label;
    * the section text IN FULL is NOT STORED, under either publisher's reading. The
      wording is identified only by the SHA-256 of its normalised text, and the table
      that holds that digest has no prose column at all;
    * a BOUNDED QUOTED WINDOW is stored — ±60 characters around the first occurrence of
      each distinct drug named, in document order, to a hard cap of 25% of the section's
      characters. The cap is enforced by a deferred database constraint trigger rather
      than by convention, because the failure mode of an intended-only bound here is
      silent and additive. Measured over the 2026-08-22 openFDA export, 20.5% of a
      section is stored on average.

  DailyMed is read only for the ACTIVE-INGREDIENT structure of labels whose subject drug
  openFDA does not identify: element names, HL7 classCodes and UNII codes. No prose is
  read from it, and the section text drugref quotes comes from the CC0 export.

  Nothing derived from this source states that two drugs INTERACT. It states that a
  label's interactions section names both of them, which is a weaker claim and is kept
  in its own read path for that reason.
